# Where `npm install -g` puts things.
#
# Without this it puts them in /usr/lib/node_modules, which pacman owns: the
# npm package itself lives there. A global install writes into a directory
# under package management, the next `pacman -Syu` that touches npm writes over
# it, and `pacman -Qkk npm` starts reporting altered files.
#
# Arch does not prevent this the way it prevents the same mistake with pip.
# There, /usr/lib/python3.x/EXTERNALLY-MANAGED makes pip refuse and name the
# pacman package to use instead. npm has no equivalent, so the prefix is set
# here and nothing else is protecting it.
prefix=${HOME}/.local
